======================================================================
PART 5: Independent pairs as matching for general n
======================================================================

n=4: 3 runners, max matching = 1 pairs + 1 singleton
  CRT 4=2×2: 1 pairs + 1 singletons
    pairs: [(1, 3)]
    singletons: [2]

n=5: 4 runners, max matching = 2 pairs + 0 singleton
  n=5 is prime: no CRT decomposition

n=6: 5 runners, max matching = 2 pairs + 1 singleton
  CRT 6=2×3: 2 pairs + 1 singletons
    pairs: [(1, 4), (2, 5)]
    singletons: [3]

n=7: 6 runners, max matching = 3 pairs + 0 singleton
  n=7 is prime: no CRT decomposition

n=8: 7 runners, max matching = 3 pairs + 1 singleton
  CRT 8=2×4: 3 pairs + 1 singletons
    pairs: [(1, 5), (2, 6), (3, 7)]
    singletons: [4]

n=14: 13 runners, max matching = 6 pairs + 1 singleton
  CRT 14=2×7: 6 pairs + 1 singletons
    pairs: [(1, 8), (2, 9), (3, 10), (4, 11), (5, 12), (6, 13)]
    singletons: [7]

n=18: 17 runners, max matching = 8 pairs + 1 singleton
  CRT 18=2×9: 8 pairs + 1 singletons
    pairs: [(1, 10), (2, 11), (3, 12), (4, 13), (5, 14), (6, 15), (7, 16), (8, 17)]
    singletons: [9]

THE MATCHING THEOREM:
  For composite n=p·q: runners group into q residue classes mod q.
  Each class has p-1 or p runners. Classes of size ≥ 2 give pairs.
  The pairs form a maximum independent matching of ⌊(n-1)/2⌋ pairs.

  For n=14=2×7: 6 pairs + 1 singleton = 6 channels + apex
  For n=18=2×9: 8 pairs + 1 singleton = 8 channels + apex
  For prime n: no natural CRT pairing; use other matching strategies

======================================================================
GRAND SYNTHESIS
======================================================================

A tournament's iso class is determined by its INDEPENDENT PAIRS.
The 'dependent' arcs are scaffolding — constrained by shared vertices.

For LRC at n=14:
  6 CRT pairs = 6 independent channels
  Each channel contributes independently to the resonance debt
  The cross-channel terms are small (runners are coprime mod 7)
  The proof reduces to: BOUND EACH CHANNEL INDEPENDENTLY

  Within each channel (a, a+7):
    The pair resonance has closed form (Bernoulli B₂)
    The pair contributes debt ∝ B₂({(a-a-7)/14}) = B₂({-1/2}) = B₂(1/2) = -1/12
    Each channel's debt ≈ -(1/12) × outside_factor
    6 channels × (-1/12) ≈ -1/2 of the credit
    Plus higher-order terms...

  THE INITIAL SEGMENT IS EXTREMAL because:
  Its independent pairs have MAXIMUM destructive interference
  (the AP creates perfect phase alignment in all channels)
  Non-AP speed sets break this alignment, reducing the debt

